# STATUS — `hmd-lsp`, the language server

`tools/hmd-lsp` — the language server, as a Rust program. It tells an editor
what each reference in an open card resolves to, over the Language Server
Protocol. It resolves through the Rust crate in `tools/hmd-rs-core`, and it
keeps no resolution code of its own. It resolves and does not lint.

Beside this file: the format itself in [`lang.hmd`](lang.hmd), the reference
implementation in [`hmd.hmd`](hmd.hmd), the TypeScript implementation in
[`ts-core.hmd`](ts-core.hmd), its editor in [`vsc-ext.hmd`](vsc-ext.hmd), the
Rust resolver crate in [`rs-core.hmd`](rs-core.hmd), the semantic-index builder
in [`semantics.hmd`](semantics.hmd), and the website in
[`pages.hmd`](pages.hmd).

## Status

**Released as 0.1.0 on 2026-11-25.** The program builds, and it answers
semantic tokens, go to definition, hover, and diagnostics for the three ways a
reference fails to resolve. It reads the text in the editor in front of the
text on disk, so a reference to a card that is not saved yet resolves. 51
tests: the unit tests, 26 tests that speak the protocol to a server on a
thread, and 4 that run the built program.

The program is on the GitHub release `lsp-v0.1.0` for Linux and macOS on
x86-64 and ARM64 and for Windows on x86-64, and the package is
`hypermarkdown-lsp` on crates.io. The VS Code extension is a client of it,
written and not yet released. The pinned toolchain, 0.6.0, carries the program for
all five platforms, so the next release of the extension bundles it. Until
that release a user downloads the program or builds it with `cargo install
hypermarkdown-lsp`, and puts it on `PATH`.

The server replaces the Python language server that was decided earlier and
never built. Python stays the canonical implementation and the only complete
linter.

## Open

| Status | Work | Notes | References |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| todo | **Go to a heading or a block.** A reference with a fragment leads to the top of the card today. It should lead to the heading or the block the fragment names | The resolver crate reads no heading and no block anchor. The rule that turns a heading's text into the name a fragment matches is the language's, so the code belongs in the crate | [[hmd-0033#go-to-definition]] |
| todo | **Tokens for block anchors and frontmatter.** A trailing `^block-id` and the frontmatter block get no token | The crate reports neither position today | [[hmd-0033#semantic-tokens]] |
| todo | **Completion** of card names, headings, and block anchors after `[[` | Needs the list of names a card can write for another card, which is the resolver's knowledge and belongs in the crate | [[hmd-0033#deployment-activation]] |
| todo | **Rename a card**, with every reference to it rewritten | Follows completion | [[hmd-0033#deployment-activation]] |
| backlog | **Update one card without loading the vault again.** Every change to a document drops every loaded vault, and the next request reads the whole tree from disk | Correct, and slow on a large vault. Needs a way to replace one card in a loaded `Vault`, which the crate does not offer | [[hmd-0033#documents-and-vaults]] |
| backlog | **Semantic tokens for a range, and as a delta.** The server answers for the whole document only | Matters for a long card; no card in the repository is long enough to show it | [[hmd-0033#semantic-tokens]] |
| backlog | **Tell the client when a vault does not load.** A vault with a broken configuration resolves nothing, and the server says nothing about why | A `window/logMessage` or a diagnostic on the configuration file | [[hmd-0033#documents-and-vaults]] |

## Broken

Nothing known.

## Limitations (known gaps)

| Limitation | Why it stands | References |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Three diagnostics, and none of the linter's other rules | The server resolves and does not lint. An editor other than VS Code sees an unresolved, an ambiguous, and an escaping reference, and no missing heading, repeated anchor, or malformed import. `hmd lint` reports those | [[hmd-0033#non-goals]] |
| A fragment is never checked | `[[tokens#Rotation]]` is coloured as resolved whether or not the heading exists, because the resolver crate reads no heading | [[hmd-0027#non-goals]] |
| A document under a scheme other than `file` resolves nothing | An untitled buffer and a file in a virtual workspace have no path, so they belong to no vault. They still get tokens | [[hmd-0033#documents-and-vaults]] |
| Positions are UTF-16 only | The server does not negotiate another position encoding. Every client supports UTF-16, and the conversion is tested | [[hmd-0033#positions]] |
| The inside of a fenced block is not coloured | A language server does not colour embedded languages. The grammar in the editor does | [[hmd-0033#non-goals]] |

## Done

- **The server** — one binary, `hmd-lsp`, over standard input and output, with
  `--version` and `--help`; a request it does not know and a request with
  broken parameters get an error and do not end it
  [[hmd-0033#the-transport-and-the-program]].
- **Semantic tokens** — the page, the fragment, and the display text of each
  reference, with `unresolved`, `ambiguous`, `outside`, and `embed` as
  modifiers [[hmd-0033#semantic-tokens]].
- **Go to definition** — the card a reference names, and every candidate of
  an ambiguous one [[hmd-0033#go-to-definition]].
- **Hover** — the card a reference names and the rule that found it
  [[hmd-0033#hover]].
- **Diagnostics** — HMD001, HMD002, and HMD003 with the canonical linter's
  severities and messages, republished for every open document after a
  change, and turned off by an initialization option
  [[hmd-0033#diagnostics]].
- **Unsaved documents** — a vault loads from disk with the open documents in
  front of the files, through `Vault::open_with` in the resolver crate
  [[hmd-0033#documents-and-vaults]].
- **Changes on disk** — the server asks a willing client to watch cards and
  vault configurations.
- **Positions converted** between code points and UTF-16 units, with a
  byte-order mark and all three line endings [[hmd-0033#positions]].
- **The toolchain carries the program** — `hypermarkdown-toolchain` 0.6.0
  holds `bin/hmd-lsp` for all five platforms, pinned from the `lsp-v0.1.0`
  release, and its release check runs a protocol session against the server
  on each platform. This repository pins 0.6.0, and the extension's release
  workflow refuses a VSIX without the program [[hmd-0033#distribution]].
- **The first release, 0.1.0** — the tag `lsp-v0.1.0` on 2026-11-25: five
  archives and `SHA256SUMS` on the GitHub release, built by the workflow on
  its first tagged run, and `hypermarkdown-lsp` 0.1.0 on crates.io, published
  by hand. The resolver crate 0.4.0 went first.
- **The workflow publishes the package** — an `lsp-vX.Y.Z` tag publishes
  `hypermarkdown-lsp` to crates.io through a trusted publisher, in the
  `crates-io` environment, after all five platforms have built and before the
  GitHub release is cut. First used by the release after 0.1.0
  [[hmd-0033#distribution]].
- **A release workflow** — `release-lsp.yml`, on an `lsp-vX.Y.Z` tag: the
  gates, one program for each of the five platforms the toolchain serves,
  named after its Rust target as HQL names its archives, and a GitHub release
  with the archives and `SHA256SUMS`. A run by hand builds everything and
  cuts nothing [[hmd-0033#distribution]].
- **A CI job** — format, lints as errors, the tests against the committed
  lockfile on Linux, macOS, and Windows, and a build on the minimum supported
  Rust version.

## Gates

```bash
cd tools/hmd-lsp
cargo fmt --check
cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings
cargo test --locked
```

